**The Core Web Vitals are LCP (loading: when the largest content element renders), INP (responsiveness: how long interactions take to paint the next frame) and CLS (visual stability: how much content unexpectedly shifts). Good means LCP ≤ 2.5 s, INP ≤ 200 ms and CLS ≤ 0.1 at the 75th percentile of real users.**

**The metrics:**
- **LCP (Largest Contentful Paint):** usually a hero image or headline. Broken into TTFB, resource load delay, load time and render delay.
- **INP (Interaction to Next Paint):** looks at all clicks, taps and key presses in a visit and reports roughly the worst. It replaced FID in March 2024 because FID only measured the input delay of the first interaction, ignoring processing time and every later interaction.
- **CLS (Cumulative Layout Shift):** the largest burst of unexpected layout shifts, scored by impact and distance. Shifts within 500 ms of user input do not count.

**Lab vs field:**
- **Lab** (Lighthouse, WebPageTest, DevTools Performance panel): a controlled device and network. Reproducible and great for debugging, but it cannot measure real INP because there are no real interactions (it uses Total Blocking Time as a proxy).
- **Field / RUM** (Chrome UX Report, Search Console, the `web-vitals` library sending to your analytics): real devices, networks and behaviour. This is what users experience and what Google ranks on.

```ts
import { onLCP, onINP, onCLS } from "web-vitals/attribution";

onINP(({ value, attribution }) => {
  navigator.sendBeacon("/rum", JSON.stringify({ metric: "INP", value, target: attribution.interactionTarget }));
});
```

The attribution build tells you which element and which phase caused the slow value.

**Typical gap:** a developer's fast laptop on fibre scores 98 in Lighthouse, while p75 users on mid-range Android phones over 4G have a 4 s LCP. Optimise using field data, debug with lab tools.

**Fonts are discovered late (only after CSS is parsed and text needing them is found), so they can delay text rendering and cause layout shifts when they swap in. Self-host a few subsetted WOFF2 files, preload the critical one, use `font-display: swap` or `optional`, and match the fallback font's metrics.**

**The problems:**
- **FOIT** (flash of invisible text): with `block`, text is hidden until the font loads. Hurts LCP if the LCP element is text.
- **FOUT** (flash of unstyled text): with `swap`, fallback text shows then changes, possibly shifting layout (CLS).

**font-display values:**
- `swap`: show fallback immediately, swap when loaded. Good for brand fonts.
- `optional`: tiny block period; if the font is not ready, keep the fallback for this page view. No layout shift at all.
- `fallback`: middle ground.

**Loading efficiently:**

```html
<link rel="preload" href="/fonts/inter-var-latin.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>
```

```css
@font-face {
  font-family: "Inter";
  src: url("/fonts/inter-var-latin.woff2") format("woff2");
  font-display: swap;
  unicode-range: U+0000-00FF;
}
@font-face {
  font-family: "Inter Fallback";
  src: local("Arial");
  size-adjust: 107%;
  ascent-override: 90%;
}
body { font-family: "Inter", "Inter Fallback", sans-serif; }
```

- **WOFF2 only;** every current browser supports it.
- **Subset** to the scripts you need (`unicode-range`, tools like glyphhanger).
- **Variable fonts** replace several weight files with one.
- **Preload only one or two** critical fonts; preloading everything competes with the LCP image. Fonts always need `crossorigin` on preload.
- **Self-host** to avoid an extra DNS + TLS connection; browsers no longer share caches across sites, so a public font CDN gives no cache benefit.
- **Fallback metric overrides** (`size-adjust`, `ascent-override`) make the fallback occupy the same space, removing swap-induced CLS. `next/font` does this automatically.

**Simplest option:** a system font stack costs nothing to load.
